Problem B:
[Statement]
[Problem]
You are given an integer $x$. Your task is to find any integer $y$ $(1\le y \lt x)$ such that $\gcd(x,y)+y$ is maximum possible. Note that if there is more than one $y$ which satisfies the statement, you are allowed to find any. $\gcd(a,b)$ is the Greatest Common Divisor of $a$ and $b$. For example, $\gcd(6,4)=2$.

[Input]
The first line contains a single integer $t$ ($1 \le t \le 1000$) — the number of test cases. Each of the following $t$ lines contains a single integer $x$ ($2 \le x \le 1000$).

[Output]
For each test case, output any $y$ ($1 \le y \lt x$), which satisfies the statement.

