[Editorial]
The core idea is to find the upper bound of $\gcd(x,y)+y$. Let us look closer at the formula $\gcd(x,y)+y$. It is a well-known fact that $\gcd(a,b)=\gcd(a-b,b)$ for $a\geq b$. Applying it to our formula, we get $\gcd(x-y,y)+y$. Using the fact that $\gcd(x-y,y)\le x-y$, we get $\gcd(x-y,y)+y\le x-y+y=x$. Hence, $\gcd(x,y)+y\le x$ for $1\le y \lt x$. For $y=x-1$, we have $\gcd(x,x-1)+x-1=1+x-1=x$, which is the maximal possible value. Note that the constraints allow finding the optimal $y$ in $O(x)$, while the above solution works in $O(1)$.

