[Editorial]
We can make $k$ what it needs to be using at most the last $5$ numbers of the permutation. Every other element can be assigned randomly. We can split this up into several cases. Case 1: $n$ is odd. The last operation will be bitwise and. The bitwise and of $k$ with the last element which is less than or equal to $n$ is less than or equal to $n$. It is always possible to get the final $k$ value equal to $n$. Let $l$ be the lowest bit of $n$. We can set the last $4$ numbers to be: $$l, l + (l == 1 ? 2 : 1), n - l, n$$ After the first $2$, $k$ will at least have the bit $l$. After the third one, $k$ will be equal to $n$. The bitwise and of $k$ and the last element equal to $n$ will be $n$. Case 2: $n$ is even Now the last operation will be a bitwise or so we can do better than $n$ here. The maximum possible $k$ will be the bitwise or of every number from $1$ to $n$. Case 2a: $n$ is not a power of $2$. Let $h$ be the highest bit of $n$. We can set the last $3$ numbers to: $$n, n - 1, h - 1$$ After the first $2$, $k$ will have at least the highest bit of $n$. After bitwise or-ing this with the third element, it will have all bits. Case 2b: $n$ is a power of $2$. We can set the last $5$ numbers to $$1, 3, n - 2, n - 1, n$$ After the first $2$, $k$ will have the $1$ bit turned on. After the fourth one, $k$ will have every bit except $n$ turned on or be equal to $n - 1$. The last element turns on the highest bit.

