[Editorial]
Because $p$ is a permutation, it will be divided into cycles. For every player it is optimal to move during the first $min(n,k)$ turns. We will answer the question by calculating maximal possible score for both players. Let us define $sum_i$ and $pos_i$: sum of values in the positions that occur during the first $i$ turns. position in which player will stop if he decides to move $i$ times. We will calculate them as follows: $sum_i = sum_{i-1} + a_{pos_{i}}$ $pos_i$=$p_{pos_{i-1}}$. Now the maximal possible answer for one player is equal to: $$ans=\max\limits_{0\le i\le \min(n,k)}(sum_i+(k-i)a_{pos_i})$$ Now we will compare maximal possible answers for every player.

[Reference solution]
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
long long score(vector<int>&p,vector<int>&a,int s,int k){
	int n=p.size();
	long long mx=0,cur=0;
	vector<bool>vis(n);
	while(!vis[s]&&k>0){
		vis[s]=1;
		mx=max(mx,cur+1ll*k*a[s]);
		cur+=a[s];
		k--;
		s=p[s];
	}
	return mx;
}
int main(){
	int t;
	cin>>t;
	while(t--){
		int n,k,s1,s2;
		cin>>n>>k>>s1>>s2;
		vector<int>p(n),a(n);
		for(auto&e:p){
			cin>>e;
			e--;
		}
		for(auto&e:a){
			cin>>e;
		}
		long long A=score(p,a,s1-1,k),B=score(p,a,s2-1,k);
		cout<<(A>B?"Bodya\n":A<B?"Sasha\n":"Draw\n");
	}
}

