[Editorial]
B — Array Craft hint1 Starting from a trival construction. hint2 Utilize the condition $x \gt y$. solution First, we consider making $presum_x \gt presum_j$ for all $x \lt i \le n$, and similarly for $y$. We can think of a trivial construction: $a[r, \ldots ,l]=[1, \ldots ,1], a[1, \ldots...,r-1]=[-1, \ldots, -1]$ and $a[l+1,\ldots,n]=[-1, \ldots, -1]$. The construction doesn't works when $presum_x \lt 0$, but we are close to the correct solution. Next, we will make a little adjustment: $a[r, \ldots, l]=[1,\ldots,1], a[1, \ldots...,r-1]=[\ldots,1, -1]$ and $a[l+1,\ldots,n]=[-1,1, \ldots]$. It is not hard to see $presum_x \ge presum_j$ for all $x \lt i \le n$, and for $1 \le i \le y$, $\max(presum_i)-min(presum_i)\le 1$. Thus, we get $presum_x \ge 2+presum_{y-1} \ge 2+min(presum_i) \ge 1+max(presum_i)$. The same applies to the suffix sum as well. Therefore, this construction is valid. Time complexity: $O(n)$. Bonus Solve the problem when $x \le y$. code

