2036D - I Love 1543 Idea: eugenechka.boyko.2_0-0 Tutorial We will go through all layers of the carpet, adding to the answer the number of $1543$ records encountered on each layer. To do this, we can iterate over, for example, the top-left cells of each layer having the form $(i, i)$ for all $i$ in the range $[1, \frac{min(n, m)}{2}]$, and then traverse the layer with a naive algorithm, writing the encountered digits into some array. Then traverse the array and count the $1543$ occurrences in that layer. Also, when traversing the array, we should take into account the cyclic nature of the layer, remembering to check for possible occurrences of $1543$ containing a starting cell. Complexity: $O(n \cdot m)$ Solution (m3tr0)

